<h1>Controlled Endpoints Disconnection</h1>
<p>Some tests have to take the machine they run on out of service. A driver installation needs a reboot before
	it takes effect, a service under test has to be restarted with a new configuration, a resilience test wants to
	see what a device does after a power cycle. On an ordinary test runner that is the end of the run: the process
	that was executing the test is gone with the machine. Orcraft separates the two. The test runs on the
	controller, and the endpoint application on the machine only executes the steps sent to it, so an endpoint can
	go away in the middle of a flow and the flow simply waits for it to come back.</p>
<p>The framework gives a flow two calls for this. <code>restart_endpoint</code> asks the endpoint application to
	restart itself and waits until it is serving again. <code>wait_for_endpoint_state</code> waits until the
	endpoint reaches a given <a class="text-accent" href="/framework/endpoints/#state">state</a>: <strong>Offline</strong>
	when it no longer answers, <strong>Online</strong> when it answers but cannot take work yet, <strong>Ready</strong>
	when it can. With them a flow can trigger whatever disruption the test needs, wait for the endpoint to go down
	and come back, and then run more steps on it to prove it recovered. The Endpoints group has two tests built
	this way, sharing one flow: <strong>Endpoint Restart</strong> restarts the endpoint application, and
	<strong>Controlled Endpoint Disconnection</strong> reboots the whole machine.</p>

<h2 id="the-endpoint-lifecycle-flow">The Endpoint Lifecycle flow</h2>
<p>Both tests hand their case to <strong>Flow: Endpoint Lifecycle</strong>. The flow reads like any other flow
	from <a class="text-accent" href="/tutorial/structure-and-lifecycle/">Structure and Lifecycle</a>, with the
	disruption in the middle:</p>
<ol>
	<li><strong>Delete Existing Maze File</strong> and <strong>Generate / Save</strong> run on the endpoint and leave
		a maze file on its disk. This is the state that has to survive.</li>
	<li>The disruption. In <strong>restart</strong> mode the flow calls <code>restart_endpoint</code>. In
		<strong>disconnect</strong> mode it runs a <strong>Trigger Reboot</strong> step that issues the operating
		system's shutdown command, then waits for the endpoint to go Offline and then Ready again.</li>
	<li><strong>Load / Solve</strong> runs on the endpoint again, opens the maze saved before the disruption and
		solves it. A checkpoint reports whether the endpoint recovered.</li>
</ol>
<p>The flow does not know or care what happened to the machine in between. It waits for a state, with the
	timeouts the test exposes, and either continues or fails with a message naming the state that never came.</p>

<h2 id="endpoint-restart">Endpoint Restart</h2>
<p>The <strong>Endpoint Restart</strong> test refreshes the endpoint application on a machine without rebooting
	it, which is what a test wants after installing a new version of something the application loads at start.
	Its endpoint field is a <a class="text-accent" href="/tutorial/endpoints-config-types/">fan-out</a>, so one run
	restarts several machines at once. Select the test, pick GMG-TEST-1 and GMG-TEST-3, set the Maze
	Executable Folder and leave the timeouts at their defaults:</p>
{{ media.zoomableImage("/assets/images/screenshots/tutorial/controlled-endpoints-disconnection/restart-config.png", "Run panel of the Endpoint Restart test with Test Endpoint (Fanout) listing GMG-TEST-1 and GMG-TEST-3, Update Endpoint App set to Never, the Maze Executable Folder, Width 5, Height 8, Disconnect Timeout Sec 120 and Connect Timeout Sec 300", "large") }}
<p>Click <strong>Update Plan</strong> and <strong>Run</strong>, and watch the Endpoints panel. A card is
	<strong>Busy</strong>, in purple, while the test holds its endpoint and runs the first steps on it:</p>
{{ media.zoomableImage("/assets/images/screenshots/tutorial/controlled-endpoints-disconnection/endpoints-busy.png", "Endpoints panel with localhost Ready, GMG-TEST-1 Ready and GMG-TEST-3 Busy in purple", "large") }}
<p>Then the restart comes. The endpoint application stops answering for a moment, starts again and reports
	itself before it is able to take work, which the card shows as <strong>Online</strong>, in yellow:</p>
{{ media.zoomableImage("/assets/images/screenshots/tutorial/controlled-endpoints-disconnection/endpoints-online.png", "Endpoints panel with GMG-TEST-1 Online in yellow while GMG-TEST-3 is already back to Ready", "large") }}
<p>The log of the GMG-TEST-1 run tells the same story from the flow's side. The restart request itself is
	answered by a disconnection, which is the expected outcome of asking a process to restart, and the flow then
	waits for Ready, takes its lock on the endpoint again and carries on:</p>
{% ansiOutput "endpoint-restart-run.ansi" %}
<p>Both runs pass, one per machine, with the checkpoint confirming that the maze saved before the restart was
	solved after it:</p>
{{ media.zoomableImage("/assets/images/screenshots/tutorial/controlled-endpoints-disconnection/restart-runs-tree.png", "Execution tree with the Endpoint Restart test at 2 of 2 passed and two Test Run rows, one for GMG-TEST-1 and one for GMG-TEST-3, each with the FO fan-out badge and a green Pass status", "large") }}
{{ media.zoomableImage("/assets/images/screenshots/tutorial/controlled-endpoints-disconnection/endpoints-after-restart.png", "Endpoints panel after the run with all three cards Ready", "large") }}
<p>A restarted endpoint application comes back as the same endpoint: the card keeps its place, its version and
	its security setting. Only the process changed.</p>

<h2 id="controlled-endpoint-disconnection">Controlled Endpoint Disconnection</h2>
<p>The <strong>Controlled Endpoint Disconnection</strong> test covers the disruptions the framework does not
	trigger itself: a reboot of the machine, or anything done to the endpoint by hand. Its panel adds the
	disruption's own controls to the same fields:</p>
{{ media.zoomableImage("/assets/images/screenshots/tutorial/controlled-endpoints-disconnection/disconnection-config.png", "Run panel of the Controlled Endpoint Disconnection test with Test Endpoint GMG-TEST-3, the Maze Executable Folder, Width 5, Height 8, an unchecked Enable Reboot box, Reboot Delay Sec 0, Disconnect Timeout Sec 60 and Connect Timeout Sec 300", "large") }}
<ul>
	<li><strong>Enable Reboot</strong> chooses who disrupts the endpoint. Off, the default, the test waits for
		you to do it; on, the Trigger Reboot step runs the operating system's restart command on the machine,
		after <strong>Reboot Delay Sec</strong>.</li>
	<li><strong>Disconnect Timeout Sec</strong> bounds the first wait: for the endpoint to go Offline when it
		reboots, or to come back Online when you restart it by hand.</li>
	<li><strong>Connect Timeout Sec</strong> bounds the wait for Ready. It has to cover the whole boot, the
		endpoint application's start and the time it takes to become reachable again; five minutes is the
		default.</li>
</ul>

<h3 id="the-manual-exercise">The manual exercise</h3>
<p>Run the test with the box unchecked, on GMG-TEST-3 here. The flow saves its maze, the Trigger Reboot step
	reports that it did nothing, and the flow starts waiting for the endpoint to come back Online:</p>
{% ansiOutput "disconnection-manual-run.ansi" %}
<p>This is your cue. Go to the machine, close the endpoint application and start it again, within the minute
	the Disconnect Timeout allows. The card in the Endpoints panel goes Offline, in grey, while the application
	is down, Online while it starts, and Ready when it is serving again, and the flow follows the same states:
	it sees Online, waits for Ready, takes its lock on the endpoint again and runs Load / Solve on the saved
	maze. Leave the application closed for longer than the timeout and the run ends in an error naming the state
	that never came, which is the same message a machine that failed to come back would produce.</p>

<h3 id="the-reboot">The reboot</h3>
<p>With <strong>Enable Reboot</strong> on, nothing is left to you. The Trigger Reboot step issues the restart
	command, the flow waits for Offline and then for Ready, and the rest is as above. What makes the round trip
	possible is the endpoint's <a class="text-accent" href="/setup/endpoints/#auto-start">auto-start</a>
	registration from the setup guide: the endpoint application comes back by itself after the machine's logon,
	so nobody has to start anything. Run it on a machine of your own that is free to reboot and set to log on
	automatically, and watch the card go Offline, in grey, and return through Online to Ready.</p>

<p>The next page, <a class="text-accent" href="/tutorial/asynchronous-steps/">Handling Asynchronous Steps</a>,
	stays on the endpoint but keeps it running: it starts work there and comes back for the result later.</p>
